### Open a chat with the widget's agent — no token, the Origin header is the credential
POST http://localhost:8080/api/public/widgets/wgt_9f2c1a7b4e6d8012/chat?language=uz-UZ
Origin: https://shop.example.uz
Content-Type: application/json

### The answer carries sessionKey ("cht_..."), the greeting and how many messages are allowed

### Say something
POST http://localhost:8080/api/public/widgets/wgt_9f2c1a7b4e6d8012/chat/cht_4d1f8a92b6c30e57a1b2c3d4
Origin: https://shop.example.uz
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "text": "Salom, ikkita lavash buyurtma qilmoqchiman"
}

### The same conversation continues — the stage and what the tools recorded are read back
### from the database, so this may land on any instance
POST http://localhost:8080/api/public/widgets/wgt_9f2c1a7b4e6d8012/chat/cht_4d1f8a92b6c30e57a1b2c3d4
Origin: https://shop.example.uz
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "text": "Yunusobod, 12-kvartal, 5-uy, 42-xonadon"
}

### A foreign origin is refused the same way the call widget refuses it
POST http://localhost:8080/api/public/widgets/wgt_9f2c1a7b4e6d8012/chat
Origin: https://not-the-customers-site.example
Content-Type: application/json

### --- Telegram (E.4) ---

### 1. The bot token goes in as a company secret, once
POST http://localhost:8080/api/secrets
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{auth_token}}

{
  "key": "TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN",
  "value": "123456:AA-the-token-BotFather-gave-you",
  "description": "@my_shop_bot token"
}

### 2. Register the bot against an agent — the token is named, never sent here again
POST http://localhost:8080/api/telegram-bots
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{auth_token}}

{
  "agentId": 42,
  "name": "@my_shop_bot",
  "tokenSecretKey": "TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN",
  "language": "uz-UZ"
}

### 3. Tell Telegram where to send updates (https and publicly reachable)
POST http://localhost:8080/api/telegram-bots/1/webhook
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{auth_token}}

{
  "publicBaseUrl": "https://voice.example.uz"
}

### 4. What Telegram then posts — this is what its servers send, shown for local testing
POST http://localhost:8080/api/public/telegram/1
Content-Type: application/json
X-Telegram-Bot-Api-Secret-Token: {{telegram_webhook_secret}}

{
  "update_id": 1,
  "message": {
    "message_id": 7,
    "chat": { "id": 987654321, "type": "private" },
    "text": "Salom, ikkita lavash buyurtma qilmoqchiman"
  }
}

### Bots this company has
GET http://localhost:8080/api/telegram-bots
Authorization: Bearer {{auth_token}}

### Remove the bot and stop its updates at Telegram's end
DELETE http://localhost:8080/api/telegram-bots/1
Authorization: Bearer {{auth_token}}

### --- Panel: conversations (read-only) ---

### Latest finished Telegram conversations of one agent
POST http://localhost:8080/api/chats/list
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{auth_token}}

{
  "page": 0,
  "size": 20,
  "orders": { "LAST_MESSAGE_AT": "DESC" },
  "aiAgentId": 42,
  "channel": "TELEGRAM",
  "ended": true
}

### One conversation: what its tools recorded and the whole transcript
GET http://localhost:8080/api/chats/318
Authorization: Bearer {{auth_token}}
